NAME
    mv, move — Move a file or directory somewhere else, because
               apparently it has outgrown its current home.

SYNOPSIS
    mv <src> <dst>
    move <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'mv' command moves the specified source to the destination.

    Unlike 'cp', which makes a copy and leaves the original behind,
    'mv' takes the original with it.

    The source goes in.

    The destination comes out.

    The source's previous location is left wondering what happened.

    Files and directories are both supported.

ALIASES
    move
        An alias for 'mv'.

        For people who prefer their commands to use complete words
        instead of mysterious Unix abbreviations.

ARGUMENTS
    src
        The file or directory you wish to move.

        Also known as: "the thing that is about to leave."

    dst
        The destination path.

        Also known as: "the thing's new address."

BEHAVIOR
    The source is moved using the filesystem's move operation.

    Depending on the destination and filesystem, this may be a simple
    rename or a more involved move.

    On success, the command prints:

        Moved

    No further explanation is provided.

    The files know what they did.

ERRORS
    mv error: <error>
        Something prevented the move.

        Possible causes include:
        - The source does not exist.
        - The destination is invalid.
        - Permission denied.
        - The destination is occupied in an inconvenient way.
        - The filesystem has decided that relocation is currently
          outside its jurisdiction.

USAGE ERRORS
    > mv old.txt

        Usage: mv <src> <dst>

        A source without a destination is not a move.
        It's just standing around.

    > move draft.txt final.txt
        Moved

        Same operation. More syllables.

EXAMPLES
    > mv draft.txt final.txt
        Moved

        The draft has been promoted.

    > mv old-project projects/old-project
        Moved

        The project has relocated to a more respectable neighborhood.

    > mv file.txt archive/
        Moved

        Assuming the destination behaves as expected, your file now
        has a new home.

    > move notes.txt important/
        Moved

        Same as 'mv', but nobody has to remember what "mv" means.

FUN FACTS
    - 'mv' works with both files and directories.
    - Unlike 'cp', the original source does not remain behind.
    - The command delegates the actual move to Python's
      filesystem utilities.
    - There is no confirmation prompt.
    - There is no undo command.
    - 'move' exists for users who believe vowels are important.

